def add_related(p,h):
    h('2 4 Adaptive computation and sequential learning',2)
    p('Adaptive Computation Time learns how many recurrent computation steps to use [16], and PonderNet learns an adaptive halting distribution [17]. These establish learned computation allocation as an older principle. CRAVE applies value estimation to externally executed reasoning and tool actions while retaining a frozen language backbone. It does not learn an internal halting mechanism or reduce backbone training cost. Self-consistency samples multiple reasoning paths and aggregates their answers [20]; this is a distinct way to spend inference computation. The implemented confidence rule does not perform that aggregation, so these results do not compare CRAVE with self-consistency.')
    p('DAgger addresses the shift between training states and states induced by a learned sequential policy through iterative data aggregation [18]. CRAVE shares the motivation for collecting states under a changing controller, but its two collection rounds supervise branch returns under the same fixed continuation μ0. This procedure does not inherit DAgger guarantees and does not remove the mismatch between label continuation and deployment policy.')
    h('2 5 Retrieval context and uncertainty',2)
    p('Self-RAG trains a language model to retrieve and critique using reflection tokens [19]. CRAVE instead trains a separate small value controller over a frozen language model and retriever. Lost in the Middle shows that access to relevant long-context information need not ensure its effective use [21]. This distinction matters here because passage-title recall and answer correctness measure different parts of the system; the retrieval proxy cannot establish reasoning success.')
    p('Calibration concerns whether numerical confidence corresponds to correctness frequency [22]. Semantic entropy estimates uncertainty over meanings across sampled generations [23]. CRAVE uses a single verbalized confidence field and summary similarity as inexpensive monitoring features. Neither feature implements semantic entropy or establishes calibrated uncertainty, and the finite-K diagnostic proportions cannot replace a sufficiently sampled calibration study.')

def add_method(p,figure):
    p('Figure 1 separates offline supervision from online action selection. Gold answers enter only the offline scoring path. Deployment uses public state features, learned action values, and the hard feasibility mask; auxiliary interaction heads provide training supervision rather than additional utility bonuses.')
    figure(ASSETS/'method_workflow.png',5.75,'Figure 1  CRAVE training and deployment. Eligible actions share the saved state and matched continuation seeds. Offline outcomes supervise the controller. Deployment uses a frozen backbone and encoder with a hard feasibility mask. This is an algorithm schematic.')

def add_analyses(p,h,figure):
    with open(ASSETS/'action_usage.csv',encoding='utf-8') as f:actions=list(csv.DictReader(f))
    count={(r['dataset'],r['method'],r['action']):float(r['mean_count']) for r in actions}
    h('7 7 Action allocation and resource components',2)
    p('We reconstructed action frequencies and cost components from the same 480 expanded-test episodes. For action counts, seeds were averaged within each question before averaging the twenty questions per dataset. Figure 4 uses ten thousand question-cluster bootstrap resamples with visualization seed 20261007. These analyses describe the completed test; they introduce no new controller tuning or independent observations.')
    figure(ASSETS/'action_usage.png',6.25,'Figure 4  Mean executed actions per episode in the expanded test. Points are task means and horizontal bars are 95% question-cluster bootstrap intervals. Each dataset contains twenty questions with three seeds per question. Counts include the terminal STOP action. A zero count is plotted at the origin; it does not represent missing data.')
    p(f'CRAVE executed {count[("HotpotQA","CRAVE","THINK")]:.2f} THINK actions per HotpotQA episode and {count[("2WikiMultiHopQA","CRAVE","THINK")]:.2f} per 2Wiki episode, compared with {count[("HotpotQA","confidence","THINK")]:.2f} and {count[("2WikiMultiHopQA","confidence","THINK")]:.2f} for confidence. This action-level accounting locates the observed additional generation in repeated reasoning calls. It is a description of behavior, not a causal identification of why the learned controller preferred those calls.')
    figure(ASSETS/'resource_decomposition.png',6.25,'Figure 5  Decomposition of mean normalized composite cost. Stacks show output tokens divided by 4096, retrieval calls divided by 4, verification calls divided by 2, and recorded logical seconds divided by 60. Stack totals equal the recorded normalized cost for all 480 episodes before averaging. The display uses the implemented reference scales rather than monetary prices or a cold-latency benchmark; it shows means without uncertainty intervals.')
    p('Figure 5 explains why token cost and composite cost can rank policies differently. The fixed schedule pays a verification component of 0.5 per episode, whereas CRAVE avoids verification in this test. The logical-time term also contributes to the composite total. These reference-weighted terms should be inspected alongside raw output tokens; their sum is not a direct estimate of deployment price or energy use.')
    figure(ASSETS/'paired_effect_intervals.png',6.25,'Figure 6  Paired CRAVE minus comparator effects in the expanded test. Points and 95% task-cluster bootstrap intervals use the already frozen statistics output. Units remain separate: exact-match percentage points, generated output tokens, and normalized composite cost. Positive accuracy differences favor CRAVE; positive cost differences favor the comparator. Vertical dashed lines mark zero. Bootstrap intervals are not simultaneous intervals; Holm-adjusted permutation p values govern the reported exploratory tests.')
    p('Figure 6 retains the paired comparison for each control rather than inferring a difference from overlapping marginal intervals. The output-token intervals are positive for all six comparisons, while the composite-cost intervals are negative against the fixed schedule. Accuracy intervals can touch or exclude zero even when the discrete paired permutation test is not significant after multiplicity correction. Both uncertainty summaries must therefore be read with the stated task counts and statistical family.')
